tx_cb_ptr = ring->cbs;
tx_cb_ptr += ring->write_ptr - ring->cb_ptr;
Reason:
Because `write_ptr` points to the next available tx_cb, the slot to be released is the one just before `write_ptr`. Computing the pointer before rewinding `write_ptr` results in pointing to the wrong (next open) slot, causing an off‑by‑one error and improper cleanup.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
tx_cb_ptr = ring->cbs;
tx_cb_ptr += ring->write_ptr - ring->cb_ptr;
Reason:
By first rewinding `write_ptr`, it now points to the slot that was just used. Computing the pointer afterward correctly returns that tx_cb for cleanup.


Now, determine whether the provided code snippet is buggy:

	tx_cb_ptr = ring->cbs;
	tx_cb_ptr += ring->write_ptr - ring->cb_ptr;

	/* Rewinding local write pointer */
	if (ring->write_ptr == ring->cb_ptr)
		ring->write_ptr = ring->end_ptr;
	else
		ring->write_ptr--;

	return tx_cb_ptr;
}

